	// Weighted by rarity AMONG SYMBOL NAMES — which is a different statistic from the document idf
	// this function used to ignore, and the distinction is the whole point.
	//
	// The original objection to idf here was correct and still holds: on the hugo sentence the rare
	// terms are "similarly", "adjacent", "incorrect", none of which any identifier contains, so
	// DOCUMENT idf pushes the denominator onto words no name can ever carry and taxes every real
	// match. searchNameTermIDF answers exactly that: a term no candidate NAME contains scores df=0
	// and is dropped from both numerator and denominator, so prose words cannot dilute anything.
	//
	// But plain counting has the mirror failure, measured on laravel/framework#48636. The issue
	// sentence "Model::replicate() doesn't exclude unique ID columns (from HasUuids/HasUlids
	// uniqueIds()) besides the primary key" put the gold `Model.replicate` OUTSIDE the top 12, behind
	// columnIsUnique (31.2) and three separate uniqueIds (31.2/31.1/31.1); `replicate` sat at 28.2.
	// Counting says columnIsUnique matches two terms (column, unique) and Model.replicate matches
	// one, so the two-term name wins — even though "unique" and "column" name dozens of symbols in
	// that repo while "replicate" names four. Querying the bare name `replicate` returns the gold at
	// rank 1, so the engine could always find it; the extra words of a real issue buried it. Measured
	// across the 11 "right file, wrong symbol" instances, the bare gold-symbol name beat the full
	// issue query in 10.
	//
	// So: rarity decides WHICH matched term is evidence, and the saturating cap still decides how
	// much total evidence a name can claim. Two moderately-common domain terms (getPagesInSection)
	// still beat one (Pages.Reverse) — the hugo case is preserved, and its gap widens, because the
	// three unmatched prose terms no longer sit in the denominator.
	matchedWeight := 0.0
	matched := 0
	for _, term := range q.terms {
		if !searchNameContainsTerm(name, term) {
			continue
		}
		matched++
